<section xml:id="sec-solutions-hw4" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Assignment 4</title>

    <p>
    Sections: <xref ref="section-double-integrals-rectangles" text="title"/> and
    <xref ref="section-double-integrals-general-regions" text="title"/>.
    </p>

  <p>
    Work each problem yourself first; the full solution follows immediately
    below it. The auto-graded version of the same problems is
    <xref ref="sec-exercises-hw4-online" text="title"/>.
  </p>

  <problem xml:id="ex-hw4-density-shapes">
    <statement>
      <p>
      Consider the density function (mass per unit area)
      <m>\delta(x,y) = x^2 - y^2</m> and the four shapes of
      <xref ref="fig-hw4-shapes"/>, each of which is drawn with the same
      <m>r \gt 0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
      (a) Choose the shapes in <xref ref="fig-hw4-shapes"/> that can
      possess <m>\delta(x,y)</m> as their density function. Justify
      your answers.
      </p>
      <p>
      (b) Determine which one of your choices in part (a) is the heavier
      one. Why?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="hw4-solutions">
      <p>
      A density cannot be negative, so we need
      <m>x^2 - y^2 \ge 0</m>, that is <m>|x| \ge |y|</m>. That holds on
      shapes <m>2</m> and <m>4</m>, and fails on shapes <m>1</m> and
      <m>3</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
      Shape <m>2</m> is the triangle of area <m>r^2/2</m> and shape
      <m>4</m> the sector of area <m>\pi r^2/8</m>. The triangle
      contains the sector's range of radii and has the larger area, so
      <m>\iint_{R_2}\delta\,dA \ge \iint_{R_4}\delta\,dA</m>: shape
      <m>2</m> is heavier.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </problem>

  <figure xml:id="fig-hw4-shapes">
      <caption>
        The four shapes of <xref ref="ex-hw4-density-shapes"/>. Shapes
        <m>1</m> and <m>2</m> are triangles cut off by <m>y=r</m> and
        <m>x=r</m>; shapes <m>3</m> and <m>4</m> are sectors of the circle
        <m>x^2+y^2=r^2</m>. The dashed lines are <m>y=x</m> and
        <m>y=-x</m>.
      </caption>
      <image width="85%">
        <shortdescription>
        Four small pictures of candidate regions, each on its own axes.
        </shortdescription>
