              <line at="hs4-y" endpoints="((7.4,3.8),(7.4,6.7))" stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
              <line at="hs4-diag" endpoints="((7.4,4.6),(5.9,6.1))" stroke="#777777" thickness="1" dash="4 3"/>
              <label at="hs4-lab" anchor="(6.72,4.92)" alignment="center">
                <m>4</m>
              </label>
              <label at="hs4-circ" anchor="(6.55,4.05)" alignment="e" color="#555555"><m>x^2+y^2=r^2</m></label>
              <label at="hs4-diaglab" anchor="(6.30,5.55)" alignment="sw" color="#555555"><m>y=-x</m></label>
            </coordinates>
            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="figure" text="Four small pictures, each with its own pair of axes. Shape one is the triangle lying above the line y equals x and below the line y equals r. Shape two is the triangle lying below y equals x and to the left of the line x equals r. Shape three is the sector of the circle of radius r between the line y equals x and the positive y axis. Shape four is the sector of the same circle between the line y equals minus x and the negative x axis.">
                <annotation ref="hs1" text="Shape one, the triangle above the line y equals x, where the absolute value of y is at least the absolute value of x."/>
                <annotation ref="hs2" text="Shape two, the triangle below the line y equals x, where the absolute value of x is at least the absolute value of y."/>
                <annotation ref="hs3" text="Shape three, the circular sector between the line y equals x and the y axis, where the absolute value of y is at least the absolute value of x."/>
                <annotation ref="hs4" text="Shape four, the circular sector between the line y equals minus x and the negative x axis, where the absolute value of x is at least the absolute value of y."/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

  <problem xml:id="ex-hw4-reverse-order">
    <statement>
      <p>
      Rewrite the integral
      <m>\displaystyle\int_0^5\!\!\int_{x/2}^{x} f(x,y)\,dy\,dx</m> with
      the order of integration reversed.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="hw4-solutions">
      <p>
      The region is bounded by <m>y = x/2</m> below, <m>y = x</m> above,
      and <m>x = 5</m> on the right. Reading it in <m>y</m>-slices, the
      left boundary is always <m>x = y</m>; the right boundary is
      <m>x = 2y</m> until <m>2y = 5</m>, and <m>x = 5</m> after that:
      </p>

      <me>
      \int_0^{5/2}\!\!\int_{y}^{2y} f\,dx\,dy
      + \int_{5/2}^{5}\!\!\int_{y}^{5} f\,dx\,dy .
      </me>
    </solution>
  </problem>

  <problem xml:id="ex-hw4-volume">
    <statement>
      <p>
      Find the volume under the surface <m>z = \sqrt{1-x^2}</m> and above
      the triangle formed by <m>y=x</m>, <m>x=1</m>, and the
      <m>x</m>-axis.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="hw4-solutions">
      <p>
      The triangle is <m>0 \le x \le 1</m>, <m>0 \le y \le x</m>, so
      </p>

      <md>
      <mrow>
      V \amp = \int_0^1\!\!\int_0^x \sqrt{1-x^2}\,dy\,dx
      = \int_0^1 x\sqrt{1-x^2}\,dx
      </mrow>
      <mrow>
      \amp = \left[-\tfrac13\left(1-x^2\right)^{3/2}\right]_0^1
      = \frac13 .
      </mrow>
      </md>
    </solution>
  </problem>
  <!-- The eight problems below come from a Math 53 discussion worksheet      -->
  <!-- (Discussion 12, double integrals; UC Berkeley, Spring 2021,           -->
  <!-- Prof. Stankova): its problems 1.1, 1.2, 2(a), 2(c), 3(c), 3(e), 3(f)  -->
  <!-- and 5(c), with the worksheet's own solutions, typeset here.           -->

  <problem xml:id="ex-hw4-geo-box">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use a geometric argument to find the value of
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_{[0,a]\times[0,b]} c\,dA</m>, where
        <m>a</m>, <m>b</m> and <m>c</m> are positive constants.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="hw4-solutions">
      <p>
        Over the rectangle <m>[0,a]\times[0,b]</m> the graph of the
        integrand is the plane <m>z = c</m>, at constant height <m>c</m>.
        The integral is therefore the volume of a rectangular box with side
        lengths <m>a</m>, <m>b</m> and <m>c</m>:
      </p>
      <me>
        \iint_{[0,a]\times[0,b]} c\,dA = abc .
      </me>
    </solution>
  </problem>

  <problem xml:id="ex-hw4-geo-half-ball">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use a geometric argument to find the value of
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_{x^2+y^2\le 1} \sqrt{1-x^2-y^2}\,dA</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="hw4-solutions">
      <p>
        Over the unit disk <m>x^2+y^2\le 1</m>, the graph of
        <m>z = \sqrt{1-x^2-y^2}</m> is the upper half of the unit sphere
        <m>x^2+y^2+z^2 = 1</m>. The integral is therefore the volume of the
        upper half of the unit ball. A ball of radius <m>R</m> has volume
        <m>\tfrac43\pi R^3</m>, so
      </p>
      <me>
        \iint_{x^2+y^2\le 1} \sqrt{1-x^2-y^2}\,dA
        = \frac12\cdot\frac43\pi = \frac{2\pi}{3} \approx 2.094 .
      </me>
    </solution>
  </problem>

  <problem xml:id="ex-hw4-order-triangle">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Change the order of integration in
        <m>\displaystyle\int_0^1\!\!\int_0^y f(x,y)\,dx\,dy</m>. Sketching
        the region of integration might be helpful.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="hw4-solutions">
      <p>
        The region is <m>0 \le y \le 1</m>, <m>0 \le x \le y</m>: the
        triangle with vertices <m>(0,0)</m>, <m>(0,1)</m> and <m>(1,1)</m>.
        Described the other way it is <m>0 \le x \le 1</m>,
        <m>x \le y \le 1</m>, so
      </p>
      <me>
        \int_0^1\!\!\int_0^y f(x,y)\,dx\,dy
        = \int_0^1\!\!\int_x^1 f(x,y)\,dy\,dx .
      </me>
    </solution>
  </problem>

  <problem xml:id="ex-hw4-order-log">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Change the order of integration in
        <m>\displaystyle\int_1^2\!\!\int_0^{\ln x} f(x,y)\,dy\,dx</m>.
        Sketching the region of integration might be helpful.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="hw4-solutions">
      <p>
        The region is <m>1 \le x \le 2</m>, <m>0 \le y \le \ln x</m>.
        Since <m>\ln</m> is increasing, <m>y</m> ranges over
        <m>0 \le y \le \ln 2</m>, and <m>y \le \ln x</m> is the same as
        <m>e^y \le x</m>. So
      </p>
      <me>
        \int_1^2\!\!\int_0^{\ln x} f(x,y)\,dy\,dx
        = \int_0^{\ln 2}\!\!\int_{e^y}^{2} f(x,y)\,dx\,dy .
      </me>
    </solution>
  </problem>

  <problem xml:id="ex-hw4-x-under-sine">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute <m>\displaystyle\iint_D x\,dA</m>, where
        <m>D = \{(x,y) \mid 0 \le x \le \pi,\ 0 \le y \le \sin x\}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="hw4-solutions">
      <p>
        <m>D</m> is a region of type I, so
      </p>
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \iint_D x\,dA \amp = \int_0^{\pi}\!\!\int_0^{\sin x} x\,dy\,dx
          = \int_0^{\pi} x\sin x\,dx
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = \Bigl[-x\cos x\Bigr]_0^{\pi} + \int_0^{\pi} \cos x\,dx
          = \pi + 0 = \pi \approx 3.142 ,
        </mrow>
      </md>
      <p>
        integrating by parts with <m>u = x</m>, <m>dv = \sin x\,dx</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </problem>

  <problem xml:id="ex-hw4-exp-square">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute <m>\displaystyle\int_0^1\!\!\int_{4y}^{4} e^{x^2}\,dx\,dy</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="hw4-solutions">
      <p>
        The function <m>e^{x^2}</m> has no antiderivative that can be
        written with familiar functions, so change the order of
        integration. The region <m>0 \le y \le 1</m>,
        <m>4y \le x \le 4</m> is the same as <m>0 \le x \le 4</m>,
        <m>0 \le y \le x/4</m>. Hence, with the substitution
        <m>s = x^2</m>, <m>ds = 2x\,dx</m>,
      </p>
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \int_0^1\!\!\int_{4y}^{4} e^{x^2}\,dx\,dy
          \amp = \int_0^4\!\!\int_0^{x/4} e^{x^2}\,dy\,dx
          = \int_0^4 \frac{x}{4}\,e^{x^2}\,dx
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = \frac18\int_0^{16} e^{s}\,ds
          = \frac{e^{16}-1}{8} \approx 1110763.690 .
        </mrow>
      </md>
    </solution>
  </problem>

  <problem xml:id="ex-hw4-arcsin">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute
        <m>\displaystyle\int_0^1\!\!\int_{\arcsin y}^{\pi/2}
        \cos x\,\sqrt{1+\cos^2 x}\,dx\,dy</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="hw4-solutions">
      <p>
        The inner integral looks hard, so change the order of
        integration. The region is the rectangle
        <m>[0,\pi/2]\times[0,1]</m> with the extra condition
        <m>\arcsin y \le x</m>, which is the same as <m>y \le \sin x</m>.
        Hence
      </p>
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \int_0^1\!\!\int_{\arcsin y}^{\pi/2} \cos x\,\sqrt{1+\cos^2 x}\,dx\,dy
          \amp = \int_0^{\pi/2}\!\!\int_0^{\sin x} \cos x\,\sqrt{1+\cos^2 x}\,dy\,dx
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = \int_0^{\pi/2} \sin x\cos x\,\sqrt{1+\cos^2 x}\,dx
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = \frac12\int_0^1 \sqrt{1+s}\,ds
          = \frac13\left(2\sqrt2-1\right) \approx 0.609 ,
        </mrow>
      </md>
      <p>
        with the substitution <m>s = \cos^2 x</m>,
        <m>ds = -2\sin x\cos x\,dx</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </problem>

  <problem xml:id="ex-hw4-fubini-tf">
    <statement>
      <p>
        True or false? Supply convincing reasoning for your answer.
      </p>
      <me>
        \int_0^1\!\!\int_0^1 \frac{x^2-y^2}{\left(x^2+y^2\right)^2}\,dy\,dx
        = \int_0^1\!\!\int_0^1 \frac{x^2-y^2}{\left(x^2+y^2\right)^2}\,dx\,dy
        \quad\text{by Fubini's theorem.}
      </me>
      <p>
        <em>Hint:</em>
        <m>\dfrac{d}{dy}\,\dfrac{y}{x^2+y^2}
        = \dfrac{x^2-y^2}{\left(x^2+y^2\right)^2}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="hw4-solutions">
      <p>
        <alert>False.</alert> Compute both sides. Using the hint,
      </p>
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \int_0^1\!\!\int_0^1 \frac{x^2-y^2}{\left(x^2+y^2\right)^2}\,dy\,dx
          \amp = \int_0^1 \left[\frac{y}{x^2+y^2}\right]_{y=0}^{y=1} dx
          = \int_0^1 \frac{dx}{1+x^2}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = \arctan 1 - \arctan 0 = \frac{\pi}{4} .
        </mrow>
      </md>
      <p>
        In the other order, <m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\,\dfrac{-x}{x^2+y^2}
        = \dfrac{x^2-y^2}{\left(x^2+y^2\right)^2}</m>, so
      </p>
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \int_0^1\!\!\int_0^1 \frac{x^2-y^2}{\left(x^2+y^2\right)^2}\,dx\,dy
          \amp = \int_0^1 \left[\frac{-x}{x^2+y^2}\right]_{x=0}^{x=1} dy
          = \int_0^1 \frac{-dy}{1+y^2}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = -\arctan 1 + \arctan 0 = -\frac{\pi}{4} .
        </mrow>
      </md>
      <p>
        The two orders give different answers, and Fubini's theorem is not
        contradicted: it requires the integrand to be continuous on the
        rectangle, and this one is unbounded near <m>(0,0)</m>, so it is
        not continuous on <m>[0,1]\times[0,1]</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </problem>

</section>
